C,D\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7fab227f50582e7e8963365d","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_dijkstra_prim_method_a4bca3e9_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Instead of the Dijkstra-Prim rule, a student proposes to take, at each step, the shortest unused edge of the whole network, wherever it lies. Let $U$ be the set of vertices it has reached so far. Which feature of the Dijkstra-Prim method is lost?","options":["The method no longer finds the shortest edge of the network at all.","The chosen edges may no longer form one connected tree during the run.","The chosen edges would then need equal lengths before they could be compared.","The number of selected edges is no longer one less than the vertices."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cb65f3c6a4d30775b4e76fe0","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_dijkstra_prim_method_a4bca3e9_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A network has edges $A$–$B$ 4, $A$–$C$ 2, $B$–$C$ 5, $B$–$D$ 7, $C$–$D$ 8, $C$–$E$ 3, $D$–$E$ 6, $D$–$F$ 4, $E$–$F$ 9 (lengths after the edge names). After $A$–$C$ and $C$–$E$ the reached set is $U=\\{A,C,E\\}$. A student compares only the edges at $E$, the newest vertex, and picks $D$–$E$. Which edge does the Dijkstra-Prim method select instead?","options":["$D$–$E$ (length 6)","$B$–$C$ (length 5)","$E$–$F$ (length 9)","$A$–$B$ (length 4)"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e6f69acbe251b91b72a4b3fa","time_limit":60,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_1a_or_modelling_dijkstra_prim_method_a4bca3e9_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A network has edges $A$–$B$ 4, $A$–$C$ 2, $B$–$C$ 5, $B$–$D$ 7, $C$–$D$ 8, $C$–$E$ 3, $D$–$E$ 6, $D$–$F$ 4, $E$–$F$ 9 (lengths after the edge names). Started from $D$ alone, the Dijkstra-Prim method grows the reached set $U$ one vertex at a time. Which edge does it add third?","options":["the edge $C$–$A$, of length 2","the edge $A$–$B$, of length 4","the edge $D$–$E$, of length 6","the edge $E$–$C$, of length 3"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cbb9418320db487206a4f4b6","time_limit":60,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}